# Track loading graphics analysis — 31 October 2026

An offline actual-App Star fixture recorded the groups drawn while the loading cover was visible. On Windows, Godot 4.7.2 Compatibility and an RTX 3090, the first load took 54.789 seconds and submitted 34 world preparation groups. Their individual draw waits summed to 32.951 seconds: 21.521 seconds across 12 track groups, 10.597 seconds across 20 car groups and 0.833 seconds across two later groups. A single wait reached 2.897 seconds. A draw can include prior visible geometry and deferred work, so these group labels do not prove which material or operation caused each interval. The repeat took 2.415 seconds and submitted zero groups. Instrumentation adds descriptor collection overhead outside the draw waits.

The probe observed 13 distinct ShaderMaterial source hashes. Two hashes recurred in different groups with different mesh formats or contexts; equal shader text alone does not establish identical graphics pipelines or that either draw may be omitted. The initial post-countdown AI-driving sample covered 62 frames over 530 ms, with a 17.582 ms maximum frame. It was not a full lap and does not certify first-lap behavior.

A separate matched-fixture effects diagnostic measured 9.556 seconds for the combined effects/final-draw phase, of which the first named particle draw waited 7.507 seconds. The scene, other proxies and car are also visible in that frame; this is not an attribution to that particle. The repeat drew 20 reused proxies and spent 299 ms in their waits. [Effects details](evidence/track-load-effects-2026-10-31/README.md) include the complete order and caveats.

An experimental repeat-only batching test submitted all 20 proxies in six groups. Two desktop comparisons measured 121–127 ms for the combined effects phase against 344–408 ms in surrounding unmodified controls. All live emitters were exercised, canonical state stayed exact, and injected navigation cancellation and memory pressure completed. The experiment did not alter the cold path. It uses unbounded serialization to compare resource state; a bounded identity-only key found 20 distinct process materials, meshes and surface materials and would not batch them. The experimental key is not suitable for production, and visual equivalence, context loss, other themes and Android remain untested. The [experiment evidence](evidence/track-load-effects-2026-10-31/README.md#exploratory-repeat-batching) preserves the controls, initial teardown warnings and clean fixture-cleanup repeat.

These are separate single desktop runs with fresh private App and road profiles; OS and driver caches were not cleared, and the shared host may affect timings. The fixture precompiles the track document, bypasses normal selection prefetch and uses no network. No Android result or native driver-call attribution is implied. The graphical run exited zero and reported the offline Windows certificate-store error. An earlier headless probe smoke passed before the final driving fields were added.

[Group timings](evidence/track-load-groups-2026-10-31/compat.json) and [raw log](evidence/track-load-groups-2026-10-31/compat.log) retain the observations. The diagnostic [App probe](../game/tests/profile_track_load_groups.gd) substitutes a [world preparation subclass](../game/tests/track_load_world_probe.gd) in the owned process, preserving pending groups and the original draw sequence. It records all 34 drawn groups and confirms the inherited warm cache is reused in the second cycle. The production App and renderer were not changed. Run with a new task-specific `APPDATA` and empty `BMT_API_URL`:

```powershell
python scripts/run_no_crash_dialog.py -- 'C:/apps/Godot 4.7.2/Godot_v4.7.2-stable_win64_console.exe' --path game --audio-driver Dummy --rendering-method gl_compatibility --script res://tests/profile_track_load_groups.gd
```

These observations narrow the cold-load issue to first-use work spread across multiple track and car graphics contexts. Earlier native traces identify driver shader compilation in a separate run, but no new driver calls were timed here. Improving cold loading requires testing real variant reduction or a supported native preparation approach against visual, first-draw, memory and device gates. Moving blocking draws into an interactive menu or omitting covered draws has not been validated.
